   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the penal law, in relation to unlawful dealing in real\nproperty",
    "basePrintNo": "S3403",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3403-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 13,
       "fullName": "Jessica Ramos",
       "imgName": "1046_Jessica_Ramos.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1226,
       "sessionMemberId": 1224,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "RAMOS"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §190.95, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3403\n\nSPONSOR: GIANARIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the penal law, in relation to unlawful\ndealing in real property\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo prohibit the use of non-public information relating to government\npermits, approvals or benefits in the sale or purchase of real estate\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the state penal law, adding a new section 190.95 creat-\ning the crime of unlawful dealing in real estate where a party to a real\nestate transaction possesses material non-public information relating to\nthe approval, awarding or decision of any state or local government\nentity relating to tax incentives, payment in lieu of tax agreements,\ngrants, bonds, bands, planning, zoning or environmental review.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's real estate market is getting exponentially more expensive by\nthe day. Inside information about government decisions impacting the\nvalue of real estate can be used to give an unfair competitive advantage\nto potential buyers. Like with traditional securities, knowing non-publ-\nic information enables wealthy industry experts to benefit at the\nexpense of every day renters and small home owners.  This legislation\nseems to bring the same rules for insider trading to the real estate\nindustry.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe one hundred eightieth day after it shall have become a law.\n",
    "multiSponsors": {
